    {
      "title": "Definition of Perimeter",
      "explanation": "The perimeter of any closed plane figure is the total distance covered along its outer boundary. For a polygon, it is the sum of the lengths of all its sides.",
      "id": "106_learn_001",
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "source_ref": null,
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "source_bundle_sha256": "0dc599270763ab236d8e654fc42edef2cbba49ebdab0fd58d3e4ce58da639861"
    },
    {
      "title": "Perimeter of a Rectangle",
      "explanation": "The perimeter of a rectangle is calculated as 2 × (length + breadth). This is because opposite sides are equal, so you add length twice and breadth twice.",
      "id": "106_learn_002",
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
      "source_ref": null,
      "chapter_id": "106",
      "source_bundle_sha256": "0dc599270763ab236d8e654fc42edef2cbba49ebdab0fd58d3e4ce58da639861"
    },
    {
      "title": "Perimeter of a Square",
      "explanation": "Since all four sides of a square are equal, the perimeter is calculated as 4 × side length. It is quadruple the length of one side.",
      "id": "106_learn_003",
      "content_origin": "GENERATED",
